in  my kubernet cluster one application already deployed using helm chart but i did not finding any chart in my workspace how to get full helm chart from our deployed and live helm release?

ChatGPT said:
Good question 👍 This is very common in real DevOps environments.

If an application is already deployed in your Kubernetes cluster using Helm, but you don’t have the chart source code in your workspace, you can still retrieve most (or all) of it from the live Helm release.

I’ll explain step-by-step for a beginner.

✅ Step 1: Find the Helm Release Name
First, list all Helm releases:

helm list -A
You’ll see output like:

NAME        NAMESPACE     REVISION   STATUS    CHART            APP VERSION
my-app      prod          3          deployed  mychart-1.2.0    1.0.0
Note:

Release name → my-app

Namespace → prod

Chart name → mychart-1.2.0

✅ Step 2: Get All Values Used in Live Release
This gives you the exact values.yaml currently used:

helm get values my-app -n prod -o yaml > values-live.yaml
If you want all values (including defaults):

helm get values my-app -n prod --all -o yaml > values-full.yaml
✅ Step 3: Get the Full Rendered Kubernetes Manifests
This shows the final YAML applied to the cluster:

helm get manifest my-app -n prod > rendered-manifests.yaml
This is what Helm generated after merging:

templates

values.yaml

overrides

⚠️ But this is NOT the original Helm chart.
This is the rendered Kubernetes YAML (Deployment, Service, Ingress, etc.).

✅ Step 4: Get the Original Helm Chart (Very Important 🔥)
You can try this:

helm get all my-app -n prod
But better:

helm get metadata my-app -n prod
To see which chart and repository it came from.

🎯 Best Way: Pull Chart From Helm Storage (If Available)
If Helm v3 is used (most likely), Helm stores release data in Kubernetes Secrets.

List secrets:

kubectl get secrets -n prod | grep sh.helm.release
You’ll see something like:

sh.helm.release.v1.my-app.v1
sh.helm.release.v1.my-app.v2
sh.helm.release.v1.my-app.v3
You can decode it manually (advanced way), but there is easier way 👇

🚀 EASIEST METHOD: Use helm pull
If you know the chart repository:

Step 1: Check repo
helm repo list
If not present, check metadata:

helm get metadata my-app -n prod
You’ll see something like:

Chart: mychart-1.2.0
Now pull it:

helm pull myrepo/mychart --version 1.2.0
Then extract:

tar -xvf mychart-1.2.0.tgz
Now you have full chart directory:

mychart/
  Chart.yaml
  values.yaml
  templates/
🔥 If Repo Is NOT Known (Advanced Recovery Method)
You can extract the chart from Helm secret (Helm v3):

kubectl get secret sh.helm.release.v1.my-app.v3 -n prod -o jsonpath="{.data.release}" | base64 -d | base64 -d | gzip -d > release.yaml
Inside this decoded file, you will find:

chart files

templates

values

⚠️ This is advanced and messy, but works when repo is lost.

