You said:
but if we have alredy deployed application using helm with our own modification now i want helm chart of this deployed version how to create helm chart of our deployed application?

ChatGPT said:
Very very good question 👏
This is real DevOps situation.

You’re asking:

Application is already deployed using Helm (with our modifications).
Now I want to create a Helm chart from the currently deployed application.
How to recreate the Helm chart?

Let’s understand clearly first:

🚨 Important Truth
Helm does NOT store the chart as editable source code.

Helm stores:

Rendered Kubernetes YAML

Values used

Chart metadata (packed)

So you cannot directly regenerate the original clean Helm chart with templates exactly as written.

But ✅ you CAN reconstruct it.

🎯 There Are 3 Practical Ways
✅ METHOD 1 (Best & Clean Way): Pull Original Chart + Extract Values
Step 1: Identify release
helm list -A
Step 2: Get metadata
helm get metadata <release-name> -n <namespace>
Check:

Chart name

Chart version

Repository

Step 3: Pull that version
helm pull <repo>/<chart-name> --version <chart-version>
tar -xvf <chart-name>-<version>.tgz
Step 4: Get current live values
helm get values <release-name> -n <namespace> --all -o yaml > values-live.yaml
Now you have:

Original chart structure

Live production values

👉 This is 90% real-world solution.

✅ METHOD 2: Recreate Helm Chart from Rendered Manifests
If original chart is LOST and repo not available.

Step 1: Get rendered YAML
helm get manifest <release-name> -n <namespace> > rendered.yaml
OR

kubectl get all -n <namespace> -l app=<label> -o yaml > app.yaml
Step 2: Create New Helm Chart
helm create my-recovered-chart
This creates:

my-recovered-chart/
  Chart.yaml
  values.yaml
  templates/
Step 3: Replace templates
Delete default templates:

rm -rf templates/*
Split rendered.yaml into:

deployment.yaml

service.yaml

ingress.yaml

configmap.yaml

Put them inside templates/

Step 4 (Optional Advanced): Parameterize It
Replace hardcoded values like:

replicas: 3
image: myapp:v1
With:

replicas: {{ .Values.replicaCount }}
image: {{ .Values.image.repository }}:{{ .Values.image.tag }}
Add those inside values.yaml.

Now you rebuilt Helm chart manually.

✅ METHOD 3 (Advanced): Extract Chart from Helm Secret (Helm v3)
Helm stores release in Kubernetes Secret.

List secrets:

kubectl get secret -n <namespace> | grep sh.helm.release
Get latest revision secret.

Then decode:

kubectl get secret sh.helm.release.v1.<release>.v3 -n <namespace> -o jsonpath="{.data.release}" | base64 -d | base64 -d | gzip -d > decoded.yaml
Inside this file you’ll see:

Chart metadata

Values

Templates (packed)

⚠️ But structure will be messy (base64 inside YAML).
This is last option when everything is lost.

🔥 REAL WORLD DevOps Recommendation
In companies:

Helm charts are stored in:

Git

Artifact repo like JFrog Artifactory

OCI registry

CI/CD repo

If chart is missing:

Check Git history

Check CI/CD pipeline

Check artifact repo

Ask team before rebuilding manually

⚠️ Important Concept
Helm = Template Engine
Kubernetes = Final YAML

Once deployed, only final YAML exists in cluster.
Original template logic is partially recoverable.

🏆 Best Practical Strategy For You
Since you are DevOps engineer:

Do this:

helm get values <release> -n <ns> --all -o yaml > values.yaml
helm get manifest <release> -n <ns> > templates.yaml
Then:

helm create mychart
Copy templates.yaml into templates/
Refactor gradually.

💡 Interview Answer (Very Important)
If interviewer asks:

Can we recreate Helm chart from deployed application?

Correct answer:

Not exactly. We can retrieve rendered manifests and values using helm get, but original template structure cannot be fully restored unless we have access to original chart repository.

